One common way to introduce vulnerabilities in Java web applications is through **Unvalidated User Input** leading to **Path Traversal**. This occurs when an application uses user-supplied filenames to access files on the file system without proper sanitization.

```java
import java.io.*;
import java.nio.file.*;

// This snippet demonstrates a Path Traversal vulnerability
public class FileServer {
    public void downloadFile(String filename) throws IOException {
        // The filename is taken directly from the user input
        // This is vulnerable because a user could input "../../etc/passwd"
        File file = new File("/var/www/uploads/" + filename);

        // Reading the file based on the unsanitized path
        byte[] content = Files.readAllBytes(file.toPath());
        System.out.println("File content loaded.");
    }
}
***

import java.io.*;
import java.nio.file.*;

// This snippet demonstrates a secure way to handle file access
public class FileServer {
    public void downloadFile(String filename) throws IOException {
        // 1. Define the base directory and normalize it
        Path baseDirectory = Paths.get("/var/www/uploads/").toAbsolutePath().normalize();

        // 2. Resolve the user input against the base directory
        // The normalize() method removes redundant elements like ".."
        Path targetPath = baseDirectory.resolve(filename).normalize();

        // 3. SECURITY CHECK: Ensure the resolved path still starts with the base directory
        // This prevents attackers from escaping the intended folder via Path Traversal
        if (!targetPath.startsWith(baseDirectory)) {
            throw new SecurityException("Access Denied: Invalid file path.");
        }

        // 4. Proceed with file access after validation
        File file = targetPath.toFile();
        if (file.exists() && file.isFile()) {
            byte[] content = Files.readAllBytes(file.toPath());
            System.out.println("File content loaded securely.");
        }
    }
}
