<div class="container">
    <h1>Project Proposal: Adaptive Neuro-Interface & Sensory Integration (Project HELMS)</h1>

    <div class="meta-info">
        <strong>Principal Investigator:</strong> Dr. Elena Vance<br>
        <strong>Department:</strong> Assistive Technology & Cognitive Science<br>
        <strong>Date:</strong> October 24, 2023
    </div>

    <div class="keywords-box">
        <em>Project Note: This proposal uses the conceptual framework of the <strong>atlantis</strong> submerged city model to represent deep-sea sensory integration, and utilizes the <strong>constable</strong> protocol for secure, decentralized data management.</em>
    </div>

    <h2>1. Executive Summary</h2>
    <p>
        Current assistive technologies often focus on physical mobility, leaving a significant gap in sensory-cognitive integration for individuals with complex neurodivergent or sensory disabilities. This project aims to develop a "Bio-Digital Bridge" that translates neurological signals into haptic and visual feedback, effectively creating a seamless interface between the user and their environment.
    </p>

    <h2>2. Research Objectives</h2>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Development of the 'Atlantis' Sensory Map:</strong> To create a high-fidelity, immersive digital environment that simulates complex sensory landscapes, allowing users to practice interaction in a controlled, "submerged" virtual space before engaging with real-world stimuli.</li>
        <li><strong>Haptic Feedback Optimization:</strong> To engineer wearable tactile devices that provide nuanced environmental cues (e.g., spatial awareness, temperature, and proximity) to replace lost or diminished sensory inputs.</li>
        <li><strong>Data Security & Privacy:</strong> To implement the <strong>constable</strong> security protocol, ensuring that all biometric and neural data collected by the device is encrypted and managed through a decentralized, permissioned ledger to protect user autonomy.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>3. Methodology</h2>
    <p>
        The project will follow a three-phase approach:
    </p>
    <ol>
        <li><strong>Phase I (Simulation):</strong> Using the <strong>atlantis</strong> model, researchers will build a virtual sandbox where users with limited mobility can test various sensory feedback loops without risk of physical fatigue or overstimulation.</li>
        <li><strong>Phase II (Prototyping):</strong> Development of the hardware-software interface, integrating EEG (Electroencephalogram) sensors with haptic actuators.</li>
        <li><strong>Phase III (Validation):</strong> A longitudinal study involving 100 participants to measure improvements in "Activities of Daily Living" (ADLs) and reported Quality of Life (QoL) metrics.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>4. Expected Impact</h2>
    <p>
        By bridging the gap between neural intention and environmental perception, this research seeks to transform the quality of life for people with sensory disabilities. We envision a future where the digital environment acts as a protective and empowering extension of the individual, rather than a barrier.
    </p>

    <hr>
    <p style="font-size: 0.8em; text-align: center; color: #bdc3c7;">
        &copy; 2023 Institute for Advanced Assistive Research. Confidential and Proprietary.
    </p>
</div>

</body>
